WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.130
- [Narrator] For nearly 50 years,

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.260
this lower Manhattan skyscraper

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.880
was an office building,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.190
but in a few months it will reopen

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:12.630
as a luxury apartment
complex with 588 units.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.310
But unlike this building,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.770
1 billion square feet of office space

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.800
still sits vacant across the country.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.890
That's about 370 Empire State buildings.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.320
Converting all this
empty space to housing,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.240
might seem like a straightforward solution

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.160
to the country's Housing Shortage.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.870
But transforming office buildings

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.552
isn't simple or cheap.

00:00:32.552 --> 00:00:34.950
- It's like a putting a puzzle together.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.181
- [Narrator] We'll
explore what it actually

00:00:37.181 --> 00:00:38.634
takes to convert an office into housing.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:41.700
And if that could be a
solution for the vast amounts

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.830
of empty real estate becoming a crisis

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.838
for cities around the country.

00:00:45.838 --> 00:00:48.750
Let's head back to 160 Water Street

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.080
located in the heart of
Manhattan's Financial District.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.030
Once completed, the building's lobby

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.275
will transform from this, to this.

00:00:56.275 --> 00:00:59.100
Developers say these types of conversions,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.280
can cost as much as $500 per square foot.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.650
Six new floors are also being added

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.720
to the 24 story structure,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.670
including 500,000 square feet

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.190
of apartments and amenity space.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.080
In many cities, zoning laws

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.970
can prevent conversions like this one

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.710
from happening all together.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.760
- The city might say,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.940
well we don't want residential
buildings in that district.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.440
That's our office district,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.330
we're trying to preserve jobs

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.370
and therefore we're not going to

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.440
give you the zoning permits that you need

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.060
to do the conversion.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.830
- [Narrator] And even
with the right zoning

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.350
other design challenges
can get in the way.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.090
That's because many office buildings

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.551
are laid out differently
than residential spaces.

00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:41.460
They might have structural
columns every 30 feet

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.830
which can make it difficult
to install plumbing

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.140
or there might be windows that don't open.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.270
Developers say converting older buildings,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.040
tends to be much easier.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.110
- When you think about how the design

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.350
of office building evolved
during the 20th century,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.880
in the early part of the century

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.130
you had much smaller buildings.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.570
They weren't as wide.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.490
- In the days before air conditioning,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.320
Offices needed functioning windows

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.330
for ventilation on hot days

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.400
and to light up workspaces.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.660
These types of buildings

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.790
tend to have a similar design,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.340
a slim shape, narrow floor plan,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.900
and punched in windows.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.520
Looking up from the sidewalk,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.470
here's one in New York

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.480
and another in Chicago.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.040
Developers for say these floor plans

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.510
translate well to apartments

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.040
because interior spaces

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.870
tend to be no more than 30 feet

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.340
from a window that opens.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.530
- And with that, it actually helps out

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.780
so that the units can be laid out

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.340
with the proper light and air.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.300
- [Narrator] Joey Chilelli,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.850
is managing director of Vanbarton Group

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.160
the developer of 160 Water Street.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.290
The building conversion we saw earlier,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.870
the office first opened in the 1970s.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.460
Postwar buildings like this one

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.680
can be harder to convert to housing.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.073
- After World War II and the modernization

00:02:53.073 --> 00:02:55.290
of office building construction.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.150
You had air conditioning,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.440
you had fluorescent lighting

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.210
so you could get much bigger floors,

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.390
and so you wouldn't have
the same access to light

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.630
and air that you had in
an apartment building

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.520
or in the older office buildings.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.360
But they were much more
efficient for landlords

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.980
and much more efficient for tenants.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.450
- [Narrator] But developers and architects

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.090
say that setup isn't ideal
for apartment living.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:21.180
- If you think about a floor that big

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.940
how could you carve
that up into apartments?

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.960
You'd have to have some
very, very narrow apartments

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.034
or you'd have to have
apartments in the middle

00:03:31.034 --> 00:03:31.867
which who'd wanna live there,

00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:34.890
who'd wanna live in the
middle of a big floor plate

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.300
with no windows.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.440
- [Narrator] You can see the differences

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.200
when comparing the floor
plans of 160 Water Street

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.600
to this pre-war skyscraper example.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.010
For perspective, here's what a

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.220
600 square foot apartment
looks like in both buildings.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.440
As you can see larger floor plans

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.540
can make it difficult
to construct apartments

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.120
with enough access to light and air.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.100
To solve for this, the developers

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.250
at 160 Water Street are
scoping out three shafts

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.870
from the center of the building.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.220
- The floor plate depth

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.140
was actually a little too deep

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.746
so we cut out a series of three shafts.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.583
We took that square footage,

00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:10.295
and then we reallocated it,

00:04:10.295 --> 00:04:12.540
into other areas of the building

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.150
where it would be usable square footage

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.860
and rentable square footage.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.780
- To account for the additional floors,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.490
the structure is being reinforced

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.440
with steel plates on each column,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.480
beams and diagonal bracing.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.733
It's also getting larger
windows that open.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.970
Other buildings with large floor plates

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.280
may take a slightly different approach.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.230
Like this building right next door,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.610
180 water street.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.700
- You cut out the floor
and create a light well

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.841
to get lightened air on the inside.

00:04:40.841 --> 00:04:43.770
- [Narrator] That means more
Apartments can be constructed

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.540
in the center of the building,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.320
including studios with windows
facing into the courtyard.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.960
For 160 Water Street the changes resulted

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.180
in almost 600 apartments,

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.770
with rents ranging from
$3,500 up to $7,500.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.710
Despite the lack of affordable
housing in Manhattan

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.710
many conversions create
market rate luxury rentals.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:05.910
According to developers,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.460
that's because it's hard to
cover the costs of conversion

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.110
while producing affordable housing

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.730
for lower income residents.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.920
- These projects are very expensive

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.200
and they just don't pencil out

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.380
without some kind of tax benefits.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.120
What happens in a lot of cities

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.160
as a requirement for developers

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.560
to get those subsidies and incentives,

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.770
they have to provide a certain
amount of affordable units.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.810
- [Narrator] In Chicago,
the city is using a mix

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.150
of tax incentives and public funding

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.400
to help finance several
office conversions,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.230
in a vacant business district.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.330
All told the plan would lead to about

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:42.930
1600 new apartments,
600 of them affordable.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.500
Lawmakers in cities like San
Francisco and Washington DC

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.080
are exploring similar plans to add housing

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.240
and revitalize their office districts.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.850
Still initiatives like these only address

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.516
a fraction of the country's
affordable housing needs.

00:05:56.516 --> 00:06:00.330
In 2021, the US faced
a deficit of more than

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.170
7.3 million Affordable homes
for low income renters,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.710
according to the National
Low Income Housing Coalition.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.540
Despite the challenges, there are more

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.560
than 2,600 office buildings across the US,

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.060
that developers might view as candidates

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.740
for residential conversion.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.310
According to real estate
analytics firm CoStar,

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.710
to help make converting
Offices more cost effective,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.550
developers say cities could
create more economic incentives

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.400
like tax abatements and subsidy programs.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.029
- Some cities are getting so upset

00:06:32.029 --> 00:06:36.420
about the economic impact
of empty office space

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.390
is that they are providing
incentives more and more.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.940
- We must make it easier

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.100
to convert office buildings.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:44.933
- [Narrator] In New York,

00:06:44.933 --> 00:06:48.690
mayor Eric Adams proposed
extending the relaxed zoning rules

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.480
that made 160 Water Street possible.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.800
Changes like these could help

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.380
increase the rate of office conversions,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.140
which have seen an uptick in recent years.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.600
In 2020 and 2021 alone,

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.760
more than 11,000 apartments

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.970
were created in buildings
that once housed offices,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:09.360
a 43% increase from pre pandemic levels.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.950
That's according to a recent study

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.933
by real estate research firm, RentCafe.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.603
Conversions may also become easier

00:07:15.603 --> 00:07:17.760
as market factors push developers

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.160
to sell their least
profitable office buildings

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.840
at steep discounts.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.390
- Sellers have this idea in their heads

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.340
about what their buildings are worth,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.930
and they don't want to sell it for

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.990
20%, 30%, 40%, 50% lower than that,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.400
but that's starting to happen.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.020
They have to pay the mortgage,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.210
their creditors are getting involved

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:37.920
and once that begins to happen,

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.232
then more and more of these
projects can take place.

00:07:41.232 --> 00:07:43.815
(bright music)

